#%PAM-1.0
# qOS greetd PAM stack — used by greetd (and therefore the graphical
# qos-greeter) to authenticate the user before StartSession.
#
# Fingerprint is offered first as a *sufficient* auth factor: if
# pam_fprintd.so authenticates the user, the auth phase short-circuits to
# success; otherwise PAM falls through to the password substack. The
# leading "-" makes the line a silent no-op when the module is not present
# in the image (i.e. when the `fprintd-pam` package is absent), so this
# file is safe to ship whether or not fingerprint support is installed.
#
# fprintd drives the conversation by emitting PAM_TEXT_INFO / PAM_ERROR_MSG
# prompts ("Place your finger on the reader", "swipe didn't match, try
# again"); greetd relays those as AuthMessage{Info,Error}, which the
# greeter's conversation loop displays and continues past — the
# authoritative result is the top-level greetd Success/Error.
#
# Everything else defers to the distro's maintained `password-auth` stack
# (pam_unix for auth/account/password, and pam_systemd/limits/keyinit for
# the session), which keeps the always-present, VM-testable password login
# working and registers the started session with logind.
-auth      sufficient   pam_fprintd.so
auth       substack     password-auth

account    include      password-auth

password   substack     password-auth

session    include      password-auth
